# sent_id = urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0012.tlg001.perseus-grc2:1.1.1
# text = μῆνιν ἄειδε θεὰ Πηληϊάδεω Ἀχιλῆος οὐλομένην, ἣ μυρίʼ Ἀχαιοῖς ἄλγεʼ ἔθηκε, πολλὰς δʼ ἰφθίμους ψυχὰς Ἄϊδι προΐαψεν ἡρώων, αὐτοὺς δὲ ἑλώρια τεῦχε κύνεσσιν οἰωνοῖσί τε πᾶσι, Διὸς δʼ ἐτελείετο βουλή, ἐξ οὗ δὴ τὰ πρῶτα διαστήτην ἐρίσαντε Ἀτρεΐδης τε ἄναξ ἀνδρῶν καὶ δῖος Ἀχιλλεύς.
# sentence_id = 1
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = Sing, goddess, of the ruinous wrath of Achilles, son of Peleus, which brought countless sorrows upon the Achaeans and sent many valiant souls of heroes down to Hades, and made the men themselves prey for dogs and all birds, while the plan of Zeus was being fulfilled — from the time when first Atreus' son, lord of men, and godlike Achilles parted in strife.
# literal_translation = The wrath sing, goddess, of Peleus's-son Achilles, ruinous, which countless upon-the-Achaeans woes placed, and many mighty souls to-Hades sent-forth of-heroes, and-them-themselves made prey for-dogs and-for-birds all, and-of-Zeus was-being-fulfilled the plan, from which indeed first stood-apart-both having-striven-both Atreus's-son and lord of-men and godlike Achilles.
