# sent_id = urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0012.tlg001.perseus-grc2:1.68.13
# text = οὐ μὰ γὰρ Ἀπόλλωνα Διῒ φίλον, ᾧ τε σὺ Κάλχαν εὐχόμενος Δαναοῖσι θεοπροπίας ἀναφαίνεις, οὔ τις ἐμεῦ ζῶντος καὶ ἐπὶ χθονὶ δερκομένοιο σοὶ κοίλῃς παρὰ νηυσί βαρείας χεῖρας ἐποίσει συμπάντων Δαναῶν, οὐδʼ ἢν Ἀγαμέμνονα εἴπῃς, ὃς νῦν πολλὸν ἄριστος Ἀχαιῶν εὔχεται εἶναι.
# sentence_id = 44
# translation_lang = en
# prose_translation = No, by Apollo dear to Zeus, to whom you, Calchas, pray and reveal prophecies to the Danaans, no one of all the Danaans, while I live and look upon the earth, will lay heavy hands on you beside the hollow ships, not even if you name Agamemnon, who now claims to be by far the best of the Achaeans.
# literal_translation = No, for by Apollo dear to Zeus, to whom you, Calchas, praying, reveal prophecies to the Danaans, no one of all the Danaans together, while I am living and looking upon the earth, will bring heavy hands upon you beside the hollow ships, not even if you should name Agamemnon, who now claims to be by far best of the Achaeans.
